This page continues the table at the heart of E283. For each a from 870 to 927, Euler lists the prime divisors of a^2+1. The aim is to find large primes of the form a^2+1, which are those a whose row is blank. Every odd prime divisor p of a^2+1 satisfies p ≡ 1 (mod 4), and p divides a^2+1 exactly when a ≡ ±r (mod p), where r^2 ≡ -1 (mod p). Euler therefore fills the table by sieving arithmetic progressions:
- p=5 for a ≡ ±2 (mod 5);
- p=13 for a ≡ ±5 (mod 13);
- p=17 for a ≡ ±4 (mod 17);
- p=29 for a ≡ ±12 (mod 29);
- and so on, up to some bound.
The factor 2 is listed for odd a. Where the remaining cofactor is small enough to recognise, the factorization is complete (e.g. 882: 5^2·29^2·37; 889: 2·13·113·269; 922: 5·17·73·137; 924: 53·89·181). Otherwise only the small factors are listed, and the large cofactor (e.g. 1657 for a=874, 1117 for a=903, 2477 for a=915) is left out. Blank rows mean a^2+1 is prime. On this page these are a = 890, 906, 910 and 920, giving the primes 792101, 820837, 828101 and 846401.
I checked the sieve congruences for p = 5, 13, 17, 29, 37, 41, 53, 61, 73, 89, 97, 101, 109 and 113 across the whole page: all occurrences are present and in the right rows. I also verified the explicit products for 871, 874, 884, 886, 889, 891, 893, 896, 899, 900, 905, 911, 915, 919, 922 and 924; all are correct. The one exception is a=914.
